<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harold Wilson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/1995">1995</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harold Wilson was a British Labour politician who served as prime minister from 1964 to 1970 and from 1974 to 1976. His governments faced repeated economic crises, devalued the pound in 1967 and announced in January 1968 the withdrawal of British forces from east of Suez, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, by the end of 1971. He also held the 1975 referendum that confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s membership of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-communities">European Communities</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harold-wilson.jpg" alt="Harold Wilson" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Courtesy Gerald R. Ford Library · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_Gerald_Ford_and_British_Prime_Minister_Harold_Wilson_(crop).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Harold Wilson was born on 11 March 1916 in Huddersfield, in the West Riding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a>, into a politically active family. His father, a works chemist, had been active in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a> before joining Labour. Wilson won a scholarship to Royds Hall Grammar School, studied philosophy, politics and economics at Jesus College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, and became a lecturer and research fellow there. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he worked in the civil service, as research assistant to William Beveridge and then as Director of Economics and Statistics at the Ministry of Fuel and Power. He entered Parliament in 1945, and on 29 September 1947 became President of the Board of Trade, at 31 the youngest member of a British Cabinet in the 20th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Hugh Gaitskell died in January 1963, Wilson won the Labour leadership. Labour won the 1964 general election with a majority of four seats, and he became prime minister at 48, the youngest holder of the office since Lord Rosebery 70 years earlier. In March 1966 he won a majority of 96. His government abolished capital punishment and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> censorship, partially decriminalised male homosexuality in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, relaxed the divorce laws and outlawed racial discrimination. A persistent balance of payments deficit forced him to devalue the pound in 1967. In 1969 he sent troops to Northern Ireland to contain sectarian violence.</p><p>He lost the 1970 election to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-heath">Edward Heath</a> but stayed on as leader. The February 1974 election produced a hung parliament, and Wilson became prime minister again on 4 March 1974. His second government held the referendum of June 1975, in which a near two-to-one majority voted for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to remain in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-communities">European Community</a>. Inflation peaked at 26 per cent in 1975. He announced his resignation on 16 March 1976, effective 5 April, citing exhaustion, and James Callaghan succeeded him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After leaving office Wilson chaired a committee on financial institutions, which reported in June 1980, and he sat in the Commons until 1983, when he became Lord Wilson of Rievaulx. He died on 23 May 1995, aged 79. His reputation was low when he left office and remains disputed: some scholars praise his electoral success for a Labour prime minister, while others criticise his political style and handling of the economy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Harold Wilson” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Promised maximum support by Portugal's António de Oliveira Salazar in September 1964, and knowing that <b>Harold Wilson</b> would not use force against white 'kith and kin', Smith rejected the five principles set out by Sir Alec Douglas-Home, and on 11 November 1965 his cabinet issued the unilateral declaration…</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodesia">Rhodesia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In January 1968 the British government of <b>Harold Wilson</b> announced that it would withdraw its forces from east of Suez by the end of 1971.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-arab-emirates">United Arab Emirates · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Huddersfield, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.65,-1.78&amp;year=1967" aria-label="Huddersfield on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Harold Wilson"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4910 5541h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4910 5541h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.65,-1.78&amp;year=1967">What was here in 1967? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
